  exercises:'First, if at all possible, listen to the phrases given below. Then using the fold-out table\ninside the back cover study the pronunciation and learn them by rote. The words and\ngrammar involved will recur and be explained in the ensuing lessons.\nCénfáíh ? /k\'e:N fa:/ Why?\nCén áit ? /k\'e:N :t\'/ Where?\nÚna aíá orm. /U:NƏ ta: oimə / I am called Úna.\nGabh mo leithscéal! /gomə l\'is\'k\'e:L/ Excuse me!\nTá go maith ! /ta: gə ma:/ Alright!\nMás é do thoil é. /m : s\'e: də hil\'e:/ Please !\nGo raibh maith \'ad. /gə rə ma: a:d/ Thank you!\nTá fáilte romhat. /t : f :L\'t\'ə ru:t/ You are welcome.\nCén1 chaoi a bhfuil tú ? /k\'e xi: wil\'tu:/ How are you ?\nOíche mhaith ! /i: wæ/ Good night!\nMar sin é ? /mar s\'in\'.e:/ Is that so ?\nMuise ! /mus\'ə/ Indeed!\n1 Exceptionally the n in cén is not pronounced. Note also in fast speech neighbouring consonants may affect\none another. The final consonant of a word may be influenced by the quality of the first consonant of the\nnext, e.g. /k\'e xi: wil\'tu:/ where the /17 of bhfuil is made broad in preparation for the t of tu. This,\nhowever, need not trouble the beginner.'
